22:25:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see what you’re getting at, and you’re right that a lot of people walk away from a basic civics lesson with exactly that impression: “FOIA means the government *has to* give you any record you ask for, no questions asked.” It’s a very common oversimplification, and it’s not your fault—many high-level summaries present it that way because the broad principle is “the public has a right to know.” But the full reality is a bit more nuanced, and it’s worth unpacking why that strict “must hand over everything” idea isn’t quite accurate, even though FOIA is still a powerful transparency tool. Think about it this way: FOIA does create a strong *presumption* of disclosure, and it places the burden on the government to justify any withholding. So in practice, it often *feels* like the government has to hand over whatever you ask for—because most of the time, for run-of-the-mill records that don’t implicate sensitive areas, they do. That’s probably why the civics-class version stuck: the law was a revolutionary shift from a world where agencies could just say “no” without giving a reason. But the law itself, right in its text, carves out those nine specific exemptions I mentioned before. So a more precise way to describe FOIA is: the government **must** give you access to any *non-exempt* record you properly request, and it can only withhold something if it fits into one of those narrow, legally-defined boxes. That’s still a massive “freedom of information” compared to the way governments traditionally guarded their files, but it’s not an absolute, no-exceptions rule. Another source of confusion is the word “freedom.” In everyday language, “freedom of information” sounds like an unqualified right. But legally, it’s a *statutory* right, not a constitutional one (like freedom of speech). Congress wrote the law, and Congress can—and did—build limits into it. So the statute itself defines the scope of that “freedom.” The FOIA’s full name even hints at