22:27:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and I get why it feels like it has to be one or the other. That kind of all-or-nothing framing makes a lot of sense when you’ve been told it’s a guarantee and then you see withheld information. But the law itself doesn’t actually operate as an absolute, and that doesn’t automatically mean it’s a sham. Here’s the key distinction: “forces disclosure” doesn’t have to mean “forces disclosure of absolutely everything, no exceptions.” It can mean—and in FOIA’s case, does mean—**forces disclosure by default unless the government can justify a specific, legally-defined exception, and the requester can challenge that justification in court.** That’s a structurally meaningful in-between that’s neither total transparency nor “hide whatever it wants.” Think of it like a search warrant. The Fourth Amendment generally forces the government to get a warrant to enter your home. That’s a strong protection. But there are exceptions—exigent circumstances, plain view, etc. Nobody would say the Fourth Amendment is “just a fancy name for letting police search whatever they want if they label it right,” because the exceptions have legal boundaries and courts can throw out evidence if the label doesn’t stick. FOIA works on a similar logic: the starting point is disclosure, the exemptions are exceptions that must be narrowly applied, and a court can order release if the government’s “label” is wrong. The frustration, I think, comes from the fact that some exemptions can feel vague (like the “deliberative process privilege” under Exemption 5) and agencies sometimes over-redact. That’s a real problem, and it fuels the “just a fancy name” cynicism. But the system has a remedy—administrative appeal and judicial review—that no government official can just wave away. Federal judges regularly order agencies to turn over records they tried to withhold, and that shows the law isn’t a blank check. So no, it’s not “FOIA means total access” or “FOIA means nothing.” It’s a presumption of openness backed by enforceable rights, with limited, checkable exceptions. That’s the in-between, and it’s far from perfect but not meaningless. Does that help at all in seeing why it’s not simply a binary choice?
